The corrected pattern has two separate responsibilities:

OnStartUp() parses mission-file config and reports bad params.

OnConnectToServer() registers for MOOS mail, usually via registerVariables().

A better conceptual AppCasting-style pattern is:

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();

  STRING_LIST sParams;
  m_MissionReader.EnableVerbatimQuoting(false);
  m_MissionReader.GetConfiguration(GetAppName(), sParams);

  for(auto line : sParams) {
    string orig  = line;
    string param = tolower(biteStringX(line, '='));
    string value = line;

    bool handled = false;

    if(param == "threshold") {
      handled = setDoubleOnString(m_threshold, value);
    }
    else if(param == "input_var") {
      handled = setNonWhiteVarOnString(m_input_var, value);
    }

    if(!handled) {
      reportUnhandledConfigWarning(orig);
    }
  }

  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

void MyApp::registerVariables()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::RegisterVariables();

  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);

  if(m_input_var != "")
    Register(m_input_var, 0);
}

The important fix is that unknown or malformed config lines should not be silently ignored. In AppCasting apps, use something like:

C++
reportUnhandledConfigWarning(orig);

so a typo like:

moos
threshhold = 10

does not quietly leave m_threshold at its default value.

Also avoid raw parsing like:

C++
m_threshold = atof(line.c_str());

because atof() fails silently too. If the value is invalid, such as:

moos
threshold = fast

atof() returns 0, which can create very confusing behavior. Prefer MOOS-IvP helper-style parsing such as:

C++
setDoubleOnString(m_threshold, value)

or explicit validation.

The mail problem is separate: registering only in OnStartUp() is fragile. If the app reconnects to the MOOSDB, subscriptions may need to be re-established. The standard pattern is:

C++
bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

and often also call registerVariables() at the end of OnStartUp() so the first connection is covered cleanly.

So the mental model should be:

OnStartUp()
  read mission file
  validate parameters
  warn on unknown parameters
  initialize config-dependent state
  call registerVariables()

OnConnectToServer()
  call registerVariables() again after connection/reconnection

registerVariables()
  Register() for all input mail variables

OnNewMail()
  consume subscribed variables and update local state

Iterate()
  do periodic work, calculations, posts, and AppCasting reports

One more subtle point: if a subscription variable name comes from the mission file, parse and validate it before registration. Otherwise you may register for the wrong variable or for an empty string, and the app will appear to “never receive mail” even though the real bug is a bad config value.

