In MOOS-IvP, silently ignoring unrecognized or typoed parameters is a major anti-pattern. If a user types threshhold = 0.5 in the .moos file, your application will boot up pretending everything is fine, while completely missing critical configuration data.

To fix this—especially when using AppCasting—you should track unhandled parameters, register configuration warnings, and return false during startup if a critical parameter is malformed or missing.

Here is the corrected, robust MOOS-IvP pattern:

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  // 1. Always call the superclass OnStartUp if using AppCasting
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();

  list<string> params;
  m_MissionReader.GetConfiguration(GetAppName(), params);

  for (string line : params) {
    string param = biteStringX(line, '=');
    string val   = line; // Remaining part of the line after biting

    // Clean up whitespace
    param = stripBlankSpaces(param);
    val   = stripBlankSpaces(val);

    if (param == "threshold") {
      if (isNumber(val)) {
        m_threshold = atof(val.c_str());
      } else {
        // Handle bad data type
        RegisterConfigWarning("Parameter 'threshold' expects a numerical value. Got: " + val);
        return false; 
      }
    } 
    else {
      // 2. CATCH TYPOS: Handle completely unknown parameters
      RegisterConfigWarning("Unhandled/unrecognized parameter: " + param);
      // Optional: return false; if you want to force the app to crash/exit safely on a typo
    }
  }

  // 3. (Optional but recommended) Verify critical parameters were actually set
  // if (!m_threshold_set) { ... RegisterConfigWarning(...); return false; }

  return true;
}

Key Improvements Explained
1. The "Catch-All" Else Block

By adding the else block at the end of your if/else if chain, any parameter that doesn't exactly match your expected configuration keys is caught. Instead of vanishing silently, it gets flagged.

2. Leveraging RegisterConfigWarning()

Because your class inherits from AppCastingMOOSApp, you have access to RegisterConfigWarning().

This pushes configuration errors directly to the AppCast HUD (like uMAC or pMarineViewer).

When you launch your community, the app will instantly flash red or show a warning count, alerting you to the typo immediately.

3. Fail-Fast via return false

If OnStartUp() returns false, the MOOS community will safely shut down or flag the process as failed. It is significantly better for an app to refuse to run with a bad configuration than to run blindly with uninitialized or default variables, which often leads to the "silent starvation" (never receiving mail) you experienced.

4. Whitespace Sanitization

MOOS configuration files often contain arbitrary spaces (threshold = 0.5  vs threshold=0.5). Using stripBlankSpaces() ensures your string comparisons (param == "threshold") don't fail simply because of a trailing space.

