Use the standard MOOS-IvP pattern: parse known params, report unhandled params, then register variables in OnConnectToServer() and call it from OnStartUp().

C++
bool MyApp::OnStartUp()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();

  STRING_LIST sParams;
  m_MissionReader.GetConfiguration(GetAppName(), sParams);

  for(auto line : sParams) {
    string orig  = line;
    string param = tolower(biteStringX(line, '='));
    string value = line;

    bool handled = false;

    if(param == "threshold") {
      handled = setDoubleOnString(m_threshold, value);
    }

    if(!handled)
      reportUnhandledConfigWarning(orig);
  }

  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

bool MyApp::OnConnectToServer()
{
  registerVariables();
  return true;
}

void MyApp::registerVariables()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::RegisterVariables();

  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

Conceptually:

OnStartUp() should read mission-file config, validate values, and warn on anything unknown or malformed. A typo like threshhold=10 should produce an AppCasting warning instead of being silently ignored.

OnConnectToServer() should register for mail. Registrations can be lost across reconnects, so the usual pattern is to put all subscriptions in registerVariables() and call that both from OnStartUp() and OnConnectToServer().

Also avoid raw atof(): it turns bad strings into 0 silently. Use MOOS-IvP helpers like setDoubleOnString() so malformed values are rejected and reported.
